  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWe start the period under a large upper ridge parked over the \nsoutheastern US, providing dry and unseasonably warm conditions. \nPatchy river valley fog looks likely again tonight, but it \ncurrently looks likely to be less than occurred early this \nmorning. Temperatures will be approaching, or in some cases \npossibly exceeding, daily record highs today through Thursday.\n\nRecord highs through Thursday:\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge           \n10-13   88(1954)       89(1935)       86(1954)       84(2017)       \n10-14   87(1954)       87(1881)       85(1954)       85(1954)       \n10-15   85(1950)       87(1897)       83(1971)       83(2021)  \n\nA weak cold front will sag southward toward our northern areas as a \nmid/upper level short wave skirts by to our north late Wed \nnight/Thursday, but the front will likely stall near our northern \nborder and then retreat north. We may see a few showers mainly north \nThursday as the front approaches. CAPE generally looks very limited, \nbut a few rumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out far north. \n\nAn upper trough digging in out of Canada will push a much stronger \ncold front through our area Friday. This front will not bring much \nif any precipitation as moisture looks very limited and shallow at \nbest, but a few showers will be possible mainly north and mountains. \nWinds will be gusty from the west and northwest Friday. \n\nSurface high pressure will build in along with colder and drier air \nbehind the front.  This colder airmass will bring night time \ntemperatures into the range where frost may occur especially across \nour northern areas.  The best chance for frost looks to be Saturday \nnight, but Friday night (probably too much wind for frost) and \nSunday nights will be quite chilly as well. After a cool day \nSaturday with well below normal high temperatures, we will see a \nwarming trend through next Tuesday with dry conditions continuing.",
